<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Holyhead</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Caergybi</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Holyhead, in Welsh Caergybi, is a port town on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lindisfarne">Holy Island</a> off the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglesey">Anglesey</a> in north <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>. It grew around a small Roman fort and a church founded by Saint Cybi in the sixth century, which became an important clas church of medieval Gwynedd. Since the nineteenth century it has been the main ferry port from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dublin">Dublin</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/holyhead.jpg" alt="Holyhead" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 瑞丽江的河水 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Holyhead_-_2024-06-15_04.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
